{
  /* UNBOUND NAMES in the browser half (src).

     The root tsconfig runs `checkJs: false` on purpose — the TS migration is
     incremental and the .js/.jsx files must keep working unmodified. But
     "don't typecheck them" also means "don't SCOPE-check them", and a free
     identifier in a .jsx file is not a type nit: esbuild leaves it as a global
     read, so it ships, and the first render throws
     `ReferenceError: Can't find variable: x` into the error boundary.

     That is exactly how «Algo salió mal · Can't find variable: models» reached
     the owner's iPhone on the Configurador (2026-08-17): a sub-component in
     ModelStudio.jsx passed `models={models}` from a scope that had no `models`.
     The Deno half already had this guard (tsconfig.functions.json, after
     "newId is not defined" on 2026-08-16); this is its browser twin.

     Deliberately loose about everything else: `checkJs` here surfaces plenty of
     type noise the migration hasn't reached yet, and the npm script greps the
     two UNBOUND-NAME codes ONLY. Both are required and TS2304 alone is a hole:
     tsc downgrades to TS2552 ("Cannot find name 'modelz'. Did you mean
     'models'?") the moment the free identifier RESEMBLES something in scope —
     which is precisely the shape of the bug this guard exists for, since a
     typo of a real variable is the common way one is written. Grepping TS2304
     alone let `models` → `modelz` ship green.
     This pass answers one question — does every name resolve? */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": false,
    "strictNullChecks": false,
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["src", "vite-env.d.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "tests"]
}
